{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795778982,"nonce":"v/SavbwQsQ6HESNmbqfhc1DfAHQ=","title":"A glass or blur image placeholder shows its tint under a motion binding or in a 3D layer","message":"Left over from d0cae22, which makes the placeholder blur inside groups (opacity, filter, blend mode, motion()). A glass element blurs in both of the places below, an image placeholder does not.\n\n- A container with a bound translate, scale or rotation and no group. The image is not part of the ordered stream: it is drawn after the dynamic batch, with no backdrop. A glass element there blurs because the paint walk plans a glass group for it, which keeps the container in the static stream. Planning the same for an image needs the walk to know that the image is still loading, or every image with a glass placeholder loses the motion path after it has loaded.\n- A 3D layer. The image is drawn into the layer's own texture, which has nothing behind it.\n\nAn image with its own opacity below 1 and no group still draws its placeholder glass at full strength, as GpuGlassPrimitive carries no opacity.","files":null}]}